@module flutter-m06 | State Management
@summary Manage app state from setState to Provider, Riverpod and Bloc.

@lesson flutter-m06-l01 | setState | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use setState for local, ephemeral state

## setState
`setState` is perfect for state owned by one widget: a toggle, a selected tab, an expanded panel. It rebuilds that widget's subtree. When many widgets need the same data, move it out of the widget.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Toggle extends StatefulWidget {
  const Toggle({super.key});
  @override
  State<Toggle> createState() => _ToggleState();
}

class _ToggleState extends State<Toggle> {
  bool on = false;
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Switch(value: on, onChanged: (v) => setState(() => on = v));
}
```

## Summary
- setState is for local state.
- Shared state belongs elsewhere.

@lesson flutter-m06-l02 | ValueNotifier | 8
## Learning objectives
- Hold a single observable value

## ValueNotifier
`ValueNotifier<T>` stores one value and notifies listeners when it changes. `ValueListenableBuilder` rebuilds only the part of the UI that depends on it.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

final counter = ValueNotifier<int>(0);

Widget view() => ValueListenableBuilder<int>(
      valueListenable: counter,
      builder: (_, value, __) => Text('Count: $value'),
    );

void increment() => counter.value++;
```

## Summary
- ValueNotifier is a lightweight observable.
- Dispose notifiers you create.

@lesson flutter-m06-l03 | ChangeNotifier | 8
## Learning objectives
- Model state with methods and notifyListeners

## ChangeNotifier
`ChangeNotifier` lets a class hold several fields and call `notifyListeners()` after changes. Widgets listen with `ListenableBuilder` (or Provider).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Cart extends ChangeNotifier {
  final _items = <String>[];
  List<String> get items => List.unmodifiable(_items);
  void add(String item) {
    _items.add(item);
    notifyListeners();
  }
}

Widget badge(Cart cart) => ListenableBuilder(
      listenable: cart,
      builder: (_, __) => Text('${cart.items.length} items'),
    );
```

## Summary
- Call notifyListeners after mutations.
- Expose read-only views of state.

@lesson flutter-m06-l04 | Provider | 8
## Learning objectives
- Share objects down the tree with Provider

## Provider
The `provider` package injects objects (often ChangeNotifiers) into the tree. `context.watch<T>()` rebuilds when T changes; `context.read<T>()` gets it without listening (use in callbacks).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:provider/provider.dart';

class Counter extends ChangeNotifier {
  int value = 0;
  void inc() { value++; notifyListeners(); }
}

Widget app() => ChangeNotifierProvider(
      create: (_) => Counter(),
      child: MaterialApp(home: Builder(builder: (context) => Scaffold(
        body: Center(child: Text('${context.watch<Counter>().value}')),
        floatingActionButton: FloatingActionButton(onPressed: () => context.read<Counter>().inc(), child: const Icon(Icons.add)),
      ))),
    );
```

## Summary
- watch in build, read in callbacks.
- Provider handles disposal of created objects.

@lesson flutter-m06-l05 | Riverpod | 8
## Learning objectives
- Declare providers outside the widget tree

## Riverpod
Riverpod is a compile-safe successor to Provider. Providers are global declarations; widgets read them with `ref.watch`. It doesn't depend on BuildContext lookups and is easy to test by overriding providers.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart';

class CounterNotifier extends Notifier<int> {
  @override
  int build() => 0;
  void inc() => state++;
}

final counterProvider = NotifierProvider<CounterNotifier, int>(CounterNotifier.new);

class CounterText extends ConsumerWidget {
  const CounterText({super.key});
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) => Text('${ref.watch(counterProvider)}');
}

void main() => runApp(const ProviderScope(child: MaterialApp(home: CounterText())));
```

## Summary
- Wrap the app in ProviderScope.
- ref.watch rebuilds; ref.read for actions.

@lesson flutter-m06-l06 | Bloc | 8
## Learning objectives
- Model state as events in, states out

## Bloc
With `flutter_bloc`, a Bloc receives events and emits states. It enforces a clear, testable flow and suits larger teams. `BlocBuilder` rebuilds on new states.

```dart
import 'package:flutter_bloc/flutter_bloc.dart';

sealed class CounterEvent {}
class Increment extends CounterEvent {}

class CounterBloc extends Bloc<CounterEvent, int> {
  CounterBloc() : super(0) {
    on<Increment>((event, emit) => emit(state + 1));
  }
}
```

## Summary
- Events go in, states come out.
- Use BlocBuilder/BlocListener in the UI.

@lesson flutter-m06-l07 | Cubit | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use a simpler Bloc with methods

## Cubit
A Cubit is a Bloc without events: you call methods that `emit` new states. Start with Cubit; switch to Bloc when you need event transformations such as debouncing.

```dart
import 'package:flutter_bloc/flutter_bloc.dart';

class ThemeCubit extends Cubit<bool> {
  ThemeCubit() : super(false);
  void toggle() => emit(!state);
}
```

## Summary
- Cubit = methods that emit states.
- Simpler than Bloc for most features.

@lesson flutter-m06-l08 | State management patterns | 8
## Learning objectives
- Choose an approach that fits the app

## State management patterns
Separate **ephemeral** state (UI-only) from **app** state (shared, persisted). Keep a single source of truth, make state immutable, and derive UI from it. Any of Provider, Riverpod or Bloc works; consistency across the codebase matters more than the choice.

```dart
// Immutable state with copyWith
class TodoState {
  const TodoState({this.items = const [], this.loading = false});
  final List<String> items;
  final bool loading;
  TodoState copyWith({List<String>? items, bool? loading}) =>
      TodoState(items: items ?? this.items, loading: loading ?? this.loading);
}
```

## Summary
- Single source of truth, immutable state.
- Pick one approach and use it consistently.

@lesson flutter-m06-l09 | Reactive UI | 8
## Learning objectives
- Rebuild UI from streams and futures

## Reactive UI
`FutureBuilder` and `StreamBuilder` rebuild when async data arrives, giving you loading, data and error states. Create the future once (e.g. in initState), not inside build.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget userName(Future<String> future) => FutureBuilder<String>(
      future: future,
      builder: (context, snap) {
        if (snap.hasError) return const Text('Error');
        if (!snap.hasData) return const CircularProgressIndicator();
        return Text(snap.data!);
      },
    );
```

## Summary
- Handle loading, error and data states.
- Don't create the future in build.

@lesson flutter-m06-l10 | State restoration | 8
## Learning objectives
- Survive process death on mobile

## State restoration
Android may kill your app in the background. State restoration (`restorationScopeId` + `RestorationMixin`) saves small UI state like the current tab or text so it comes back. Persist important data in storage instead.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Tabs extends StatefulWidget {
  const Tabs({super.key});
  @override
  State<Tabs> createState() => _TabsState();
}

class _TabsState extends State<Tabs> with RestorationMixin {
  final _index = RestorableInt(0);
  @override
  String get restorationId => 'tabs';
  @override
  void restoreState(RestorationBucket? old, bool initial) => registerForRestoration(_index, 'index');
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Text('Tab ${_index.value}');
}
```

## Summary
- Set restorationScopeId on MaterialApp.
- Use Restorable* properties for UI state.
